==============================================================================
0.  FORMALISM AND IDENTIFIABILITY, before anything is fitted
==============================================================================

   Psi   slow matter      d^2x/dt^2 = -grad Psi        <- the modified Poisson
   Phi   spatial curvature                                equation gives THIS
   light                  deflection ~ grad(Phi + Psi)  <- and says nothing
                                                           about THIS
   slip  eta = Phi/Psi    lensing response Sigma_s = (1+eta)/2
   GR without anisotropic stress: eta = 1, Sigma_s = 1  ->  NO SLIP

   I1  Sigma_s = 2 as a rescaling of the PROJECTED mass: chi2 = 330.560241
       Sigma_s = 2 as a rescaling of the 3-D lensing mass: chi2 = 330.560241
       identical to 0.0e+00 relative.  A CONSTANT slip is exactly
       a lens-mass rescaling, so lensing alone -- shear, images or
       time delays -- can never separate the two.  The frozen
       dynamics law is the ONLY source of identification.

   I2  reduced shear is NOT linear in Sigma_s (kappa up to 0.040):
         R [Mpc]     kappa   dln g+/dln Sigma_s
           0.335    0.0052               1.0054
           0.656    0.0106               1.0110
           1.226    0.0077               1.0080
           2.177    0.0043               1.0045
           3.857    0.0023               1.0024

       mean 1.0058, inner bins 1.0054.  A linearised 'shear scales with mass'
       shortcut would be wrong by that much and would bias any slip.

